# Online play: a lobby, then a lockstep match.
#
# Transports: Steam (when the GodotSteam extension is installed and Steam is running) or a direct
# connection by IP address (LAN, or the internet with port forwarding). Both go through Godot's
# MultiplayerAPI, so everything above the transport is the same.
#
# Lockstep: every computer runs the same simulation from the same seed. Only player commands travel.
# The host is the clock: 30 times a second it bundles the commands it has received and sends the bundle
# to everyone, then applies it and steps the match one tick. The others apply each bundle when it
# arrives, in order. Every 2 seconds everybody compares a checksum of the match to catch desyncs.
#
# 0.25 rejoin and host migration (docs/NETCODE.md): everybody keeps the command log of the match so far, so a
# player who lost the connection (or whose game crashed) can come back: they reconnect to the same room, prove
# who they are with a per-match secret (its hash travels in the line-up), get the log from the tick they
# stopped at, fast-forward, and take their hero back from the AI. On the relay the host may drop too: the relay
# makes the longest-connected player peer 1, that game becomes the clock from the furthest tick anybody
# received, and the old host can rejoin as a player.
# 0.26 reconnection (docs/NETCODE.md "Reconnection"): nobody has to press anything. A dropped game retries on
# its own every RECONNECT_EVERY s for RECONNECT_FOR s: a player rejoins (above); the relay host re-claims its
# room by code with the room secret (relay_peer.rehost), keeps the match frozen at its tick and resumes when the
# first player is back; players who never return are handed to the AI after PEER_DROP as usual.
